---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
const snippet0 = `rows, cols = 4, 3
table = slide.shapes.add_table(
    rows, cols, *BBox.from_inches(1, 1.5, 8, 4),
    style="clean",                # disables every inherited style flag
).table

cell = table.cell(0, 0)
cell.text = "Quarter"
cell.fill.solid()
cell.fill.fore_color.rgb = "#0B5CFF"

# Per-cell borders (fork addition)
cell.borders.bottom = {"color": "#0D0D0D", "width_pt": 1.5}`;

const snippet1 = `# Replace the text inside an existing template's SmartArt
slide.smart_art[0].set_text(["Discover", "Design", "Deliver"])`;

const snippet2 = `shape.three_d.bevel(top="circle", top_width=Pt(6), top_height=Pt(6))
shape.three_d.extrusion(depth=Pt(12), color="#0B5CFF")
shape.three_d.rotation(x=15, y=20, z=0)`;

---

<DocsLayout
  title="Tables, SmartArt & 3D"
  description="The inherited table API plus Cell.borders, SmartArt text substitution, and bevel/extrusion 3D primitives."
>
  <p class="lead">
    Three smaller but powerful corners of the API: styled tables, text substitution inside
    SmartArt, and real 3D bevel/extrusion primitives.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tables">Tables</h2>
  <Code code={snippet0} />
  <p>
    Use <code>style="clean"</code> whenever you'll set custom cell borders or fills — inherited
    style flags otherwise fight your formatting.
  </p>

  <h2 id="smartart">SmartArt text substitution</h2>
  <Code code={snippet1} />
  <p>
    This is the pragmatic way to use SmartArt: design the diagram once in PowerPoint, then fill it
    from Python at generation time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="three-d">3D primitives</h2>
  <Code code={snippet2} />
  <p>
    Bevels and extrusion are real DrawingML — they stay editable in PowerPoint and respect the
    theme's lighting rig.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
